Amazon SNS — Data Limitations

Scope: Message data constraints for application-to-application use. Excludes throughput quotas and SMS/email/push channels.
Verified: September 2026, against AWS General Reference and SNS Developer Guide.

Payload size

Maximum message size is 262,144 bytes (256 KiB); maximum header size is 16,384 bytes (16 KiB). Neither is adjustable.

The limit is measured in bytes, not characters. Multi-byte UTF-8 characters consume 2–4 bytes each, so payloads that appear within limits by character count may still be rejected.

Payloads up to 2 GB are possible via the SNS Extended Client Libraries, which store the message in S3 and publish a pointer. This is not native SNS — every subscriber must use the corresponding library to resolve the pointer.

Billing is per 64 KB chunk, on both publish and delivery. A 256 KB message bills as four requests each way.

Encoding and content

Messages must be UTF-8 encoded strings. Permitted content is XML, JSON, or unformatted text.

Binary is not supported in the message body and must be base64-encoded, adding roughly 33% to size against the 256 KiB limit. Binary is supported natively only as a message attribute with DataType: Binary.

Message attributes

Attributes are metadata sent alongside the body, but they count against the same 256 KiB limit — name, type, and value all included. Supported types are String, String.Array, Number, and Binary. Each must be non-empty and non-null.

Three behaviors matter:

Attributes are transmitted only when MessageStructure is String, not JSON.
For SQS subscriptions with Raw Message Delivery enabled, the maximum is 10 attributes. Messages exceeding this are discarded as client-side errors with no failure indication to the publisher. Disable raw delivery to send more.
When an attribute is used for filtering, its value must be a valid JSON string.
Structural field limits

Topic names are 1–256 characters, ASCII letters/numbers/underscores/hyphens only, and FIFO names must end in .fifo. Subject must be UTF-8, under 100 characters, with no line breaks or control characters.

On FIFO topics, MessageGroupId (max 128 characters) is required on every message — publish fails without it. MessageDeduplicationId (max 128 characters) is required unless ContentBasedDeduplication is enabled.

PublishBatch accepts 10 entries; each batch entry Id is max 80 characters and must be unique within the batch.

When MessageStructure is json, the object must contain a default key and protocol keys must map to simple JSON string values. Nested objects are not permitted at the top level.

Filter policy limits

Maximum policy size 256 KB, maximum five keys, and total combination of values capped at 150 (multiply the number of values in each array). Numeric matching ranges from -1 billion to 1 billion with five decimal places; values outside this range fail to match silently rather than erroring. String matching is case-sensitive. Wildcard complexity is capped at 100 points.

Defaults are 200 filter policies per topic and 10,000 per account, both adjustable. The per-topic limit does not block subscription creation — the call fails only when the policy is attached via Subscribe or SetSubscriptionAttributes.

Policies must be valid UTF-8 JSON containing only quoted strings, numbers, and the unquoted keywords true, false, and null.

Data retention

Standard topics retain nothing. There is no storage, no replay, and no consumer polling. A message exists only for the duration of its delivery attempts, then is discarded.

Retry budgets before discard: 100,015 attempts over 23 days for SQS and Lambda; 50 attempts over 6 hours for SMTP, SMS, and mobile push; customer-defined for HTTP/S, with total retry time capped at 3,600 seconds.

If all attempts fail and no dead-letter queue is attached, the message is permanently lost. DLQs attach at the subscription level, not the topic level.

FIFO topics are the only exception — archive and replay policies retain messages for a configurable window and allow replay to subscriptions.

Encryption and deduplication

Server-side encryption uses AWS KMS with an AWS managed or customer managed key. Data protection policies can scan and redact PII in transit, though PutDataProtectionPolicy is throttled to 1 TPS in all commercial regions.

Content-based deduplication on FIFO topics hashes the message body only, excluding attributes. Two messages with identical bodies but different attributes are treated as duplicates, and only one is delivered.

Design implications

Attach a DLQ to every subscription carrying data you cannot afford to lose — there is no other safety net on standard topics. Delivery is at-least-once with no ordering guarantee, so consumers must be idempotent. For payloads above 256 KiB, prefer an explicit claim-check pattern over the Extended Client Library unless every consumer can adopt it. Validate attribute count before publishing to raw-delivery SQS subscriptions, since failures are silent. And treat payload size as a cost lever, not just a technical ceiling, given the 64 KB billing chunks.
